 ## First Checks
 
@@ -1644,3 +1647,84 @@ CDC ACM shell equivalent (G3 only):
 linkr-debugger:~$ vin set 1.8v
 linkr-debugger:~$ vin set 3.3v
 ```
+
+## Web UI
+
+Open `$BOARD_URL/` for the embedded dashboard. The **Automation** tab contains
+the workflow editor and Task card. Built-in rows come from the firmware-owned
+catalog at `GET /api/v1/tasks/catalog`; the Web UI never holds its own recovery
+recipes. The card lists those firmware-provided built-ins together with
+flash-backed stored tasks, runs either through ordinary control APIs, and
+stores or clears only explicit stored sequences. The **GPIO** tab uses the pinout directly:
+pin color shows the latest live level (black LOW, red HIGH, including input
+pins), while a dashed outer ring means input and a solid ring means output.
+Pins are driven by direct gestures with no selection step: a short tap
+requests output LOW once the 220 ms double-tap window expires, a second tap
+inside that window requests input acquisition, and a 600 ms hold requests
+output HIGH while a danger-colored progress arc sweeps around the pin.
+Movement beyond 8 px, lost pointer capture, pointercancel, or Escape cancels
+a gesture without writing. On a focused pin, Enter/Space/0 drive LOW, 1
+drives HIGH, and I requests input immediately; key auto-repeat is ignored.
+The card never writes on mount or refresh, is non-optimistic, allows one
+in-flight request at a time, and dims the pending pin. The UI does not infer
+output capability from a pin name or note; unsupported requests remain
+firmware-owned errors shown by the card. Async success/error feedback renders
+after the stable pinout region and expands below it, so mounting or clearing
+feedback never changes the pin/SVG top-left geometry.
+
+The **Saved configuration** card starts collapsed. Its header chevron is an
+accessible expand/collapse button that reveals or hides only the saved rows and
+actions; the title, description, and current status remain visible. Toggling it
+does not call save, clear, or refresh, and it preserves the current selection
+and in-progress feedback.
+
+## TUI
+
+Run `radxa-linkr-debuggerctl` or `rdb` without a subcommand. The normal screen is
+borderless: two status rows, an optional seven-row three-channel adaptive current
+scope, the Controls/Saved Config/Status page strip, a fixed page-specific table
+header, dense object rows, and a one-row htop-style keybar. Empty telemetry uses
+zero rows. Key/action units that do not fit are omitted whole rather than clipped.
+
+Tab/Shift+Tab changes pages. Up/Down moves projected rows and preserves the GPIO
+side when possible; Left/Right moves only between sibling GPIO cells. Controls
+and Saved Config keep the active item visible, while the same navigation keys
+scroll Status; each page retains its own scroll offset. Power/Switch rows and
+each GPIO half-row cell have independent mouse targets. Right-clicking a GPIO
+matches `i` and restores input mode.
+
+Power rows come only from the latest firmware `power_outputs` catalog and keep
+its order. The TUI has no host rail list: future outputs appear automatically,
+removed outputs disappear, and a reordered catalog keeps selection on the same
+hardware name. `5V_FIN` remains absent because firmware exposes it as an input,
+not a controllable output.
+
+GPIO grouping comes only from firmware `layoutGroup/layoutLabel/layoutRow/
+layoutColumn` metadata. At width 48 or greater one firmware physical row may
+hold up to two independent cells; narrower terminals use one pin per row and
+incomplete metadata falls back to snapshot order. GPIO uses the Web UI action
+contract and renders `◌ IN LOW`, `◌ IN HIGH`, `○ OUT LOW`, or `● OUT HIGH`;
+LOW is dark gray and HIGH is bold red. The host does not hardcode connector or
+pin maps.
+
+Every direct power action and each firmware-marked confirmation switch such as
+VIO first opens a centered red-bordered confirmation box.
+The first activation has no hardware side effect; confirm within three seconds
+with Enter/Space or the Confirm button. Confirmation overlays are the only
+bordered TUI regions.
+
+This presentation does not change API endpoints, the two-second HTTP poll,
+confirmation/action semantics, hardware defaults, or firmware behavior.
+
+## Safety
+
+- Enumerate firmware-advertised IDs, rails, GPIOs, and routes before acting.
+- Confirm target power, GPIO, VIN, route, OTA, and BOOTSEL side effects.
+- Never use the application-only UF2 for ROM BOOTSEL.
+- Never send `.uf2` or `.elf` to the OTA endpoint.
+- Do not auto-confirm a dangerous configuration request.
+- A failed built-in task stops its main sequence immediately and attempts the
+  catalog-declared cleanup (which the firmware advertises as part of the
+  catalog entry). Inspect its primary and cleanup diagnostics, then inspect and
+  restore the selected rail through ordinary APIs before continuing. Hosts do
+  not infer or hardcode any cleanup; cleanup comes strictly from the catalog.
